uv run python -m mgraph enrich-audio --limit 1
uv run python -m mgraph enrich-image --limit 1
uv run python -m mgraph enrich-pdf --limit 1
uv run python -m mgraph profile --limit 10 --model kimi-k3
uv run python -m mgraph embed --limit 10
uv run python -m mgraph relate --limit 5 --model kimi-k3
uv run python -m mgraph status
```

`zsh run_parallel_profiles.sh` applies the pilot migration, screens fetched web identities, repairs saved primary names before and after four disjoint Kimi profile shards, audits every eligible current-version profile, then runs Cloudflare embeddings and up to 20,000 distinct Kimi pair judgments. The audit stops downstream inference if any eligible profile is missing, stale, or has unsupported quotes, or if a web page has not been screened. The script resumes from versioned persisted projections. Kimi K3 profiling and identity screening default to `low` reasoning effort; set `MGRAPH_DO_PROFILE_REASONING_EFFORT` or `MGRAPH_DO_IDENTITY_REASONING_EFFORT` in `.env` to evaluate another effort level. Pair judgments use the model default unless `MGRAPH_DO_REASONING_EFFORT` is set. Run `.venv/bin/python -m unittest discover -s tests -v`, inspect `mgraph status`, and manually compare sampled claims and property quotes with `read_memory` before restarting a large run after a profile change.

Run the prototype with `uv run python -m mgraph.map_server` and open `http://127.0.0.1:8765`. The Proximity map loads connected group summaries eight at a time as the horizontal canvas scrolls; it never plots standalone entries. Each visible group loads 24 new entries per page, with bridge entries from earlier pages so every displayed entry remains connected. Offscreen group cards and edges are discarded. Persisted pair scores drive spring distances, pair feedback overrides model scores, and closer-than feedback adjusts positions when all three entries are on the same page. The grouping threshold is 0.65. Group topology is cached for 30 seconds, so newly judged pairs may take up to 30 seconds to appear. Search still covers every projected entry, including entries without a profile or graph position.

The Library tab pages authoritative DynamoDB source records by `node`, `file`, `link`, `collection`, `property`, and `linkTag`. Record details show raw source fields, associated root entry evidence, extraction output, profile concepts/tags/properties/claims, and embedding and pair-judgment status when available. The entry API also returns each extraction's identity status and reason; the Library continues to expose raw quarantined text for inspection, while MCP reads omit it. The library reads hosted PostgreSQL and does not download R2 snapshots or rerun inference. For a manual check, open the library, switch resources, search within a resource, advance a page, inspect a source record, then return to the map and open a connected node. The map is a pilot visualization, and group coordinates are recalculated within each displayed page rather than being stable global coordinates.

The local MCP server runs over stdio with `/Users/serro/Documents/dev/n/agent/experiments/mgraph/.venv/bin/python -m mgraph.mcp_server`. Use that absolute executable path and `-m mgraph.mcp_server` as arguments in an AI host's MCP configuration; no special working directory is needed. It exposes read-only `search_memories`, `read_memory`, `nearby_memories`, `compare_memories`, and `tabulate_memory_differences` tools. The last tool assembles persisted properties and cited claims side by side so an agent can answer comparisons without refetching source pages. Both in-process and subprocess MCP client calls passed. A connected agent can read the private one-user memory projection returned by these tools.

