# m graph pilot

Local development lives here. The pilot reads one user's legacy Memotron data from DynamoDB, stores derived graph records in hosted PostgreSQL, and stores source snapshots and extraction artifacts in a private Cloudflare R2 bucket. The loopback-only horizontal map and local MCP server consume the same projection.

## Pipeline

The source stage reads only the six Memotron partitions (`node`, `file`, `link`, `collection`, `property`, `linkTag`) for the configured user. Each DynamoDB query page becomes a compressed, hashed R2 object and is upserted into the dedicated PostgreSQL schema. Page checkpoints make interrupted runs resumable. The source partitions are not a transactional point-in-time snapshot. A later catch-up generation is needed to incorporate edits after this completed backfill.

The projection stage identifies top-level entries, treats the legacy `$NONE` reference sentinel as empty, resolves reciprocal page/clip references, and folds child blocks into cited evidence without shortening source blocks or dropping descendants. Public-page HTML, owned file media, profiles, embeddings, and pair judgments are separately versioned derivations. An entry's source hash and each derivation's source hash determine whether it needs recomputation. Raw fetched page and media bytes go to R2; PostgreSQL holds searchable text, structured profiles, a 1,024-dimensional pgvector projection, and relation judgments. Before profiling, a resumable web-identity screen compares each fetched page with the saved title, URL, and description. Obvious matches are accepted; title conflicts, private-page URLs, and login fallbacks receive a Kimi judgment. Conflicting or uncertain page text is quarantined from profiling, search, and MCP reads while the original saved record remains available. The screen deletes any affected derived profile, embedding, and pair judgments so they can be regenerated from accepted evidence. Profiling splits each source block and accepte…1273 tokens truncated…te one-user memory projection returned by these tools.

