README.md:3:Local development lives here. The pilot reads one user's legacy Memotron data from DynamoDB, stores derived graph records in hosted PostgreSQL, and stores source snapshots and extraction artifacts in a private Cloudflare R2 bucket. The loopback-only horizontal map and local MCP server consume the same projection.
README.md:9:The projection stage identifies top-level entries, treats the legacy `$NONE` reference sentinel as empty, resolves reciprocal page/clip references, and folds child blocks into cited evidence without shortening source blocks or dropping descendants. Public-page HTML, owned file media, profiles, embeddings, and pair judgments are separately versioned derivations. An entry's source hash and each derivation's source hash determine whether it needs recomputation. Raw fetched page and media bytes go to R2; PostgreSQL holds searchable text, structured profiles, a 1,024-dimensional pgvector projection, and relation judgments. Before profiling, a resumable web-identity screen compares each fetched page with the saved title, URL, and description. Obvious matches are accepted; title conflicts, private-page URLs, and login fallbacks receive a Kimi judgment. Conflicting or uncertain page text is quarantined from profiling, search, and MCP reads while the original saved record remains available. The screen deletes any affected derived profile, embedding, and pair judgments so they can be regenerated from accepted evidence. Profiling splits each source block and accepted extraction into overlapping passages of at most 2,800 characters, packs complete passages into model requests, and reconciles long-entry summaries and uncertainty. Claims and property facts carry an entry ID, passage ID, and exact supporting quote; a validator removes facts whose quote does not occur in that passage. The saved title is the single primary `name`; other cited names remain in property facts. Property facts retain confidence and extraction observation time, and common property aliases are normalized for comparison. Entries needing more than 64 model requests raise a visible error. The legacy vector DataFn schema is unused.
README.md:11:Candidate relations are the union of vector neighbors, lexical matches, explicit links, and shared extracted concepts. DO reasoning judges candidates and persists a 0-1 proximity score, relation type, explanation, and supporting entry IDs. The evidence profile captures concepts, tags, properties, claims, and uncertainty for later chat and comparison surfaces. Thin title-only records remain marked `thin` and should not be treated as fully understood.
README.md:33:Run the prototype with `uv run python -m mgraph.map_server` and open `http://127.0.0.1:8765`. The Proximity map loads connected group summaries eight at a time as the horizontal canvas scrolls; it never plots standalone entries. Each visible group loads 24 new entries per page, with bridge entries from earlier pages so every displayed entry remains connected. Offscreen group cards and edges are discarded. Persisted pair scores drive spring distances, pair feedback overrides model scores, and closer-than feedback adjusts positions when all three entries are on the same page. The grouping threshold is 0.65. Group topology is cached for 30 seconds, so newly judged pairs may take up to 30 seconds to appear. Search still covers every projected entry, including entries without a profile or graph position.
README.md:35:The Library tab pages authoritative DynamoDB source records by `node`, `file`, `link`, `collection`, `property`, and `linkTag`. On `node`, the `Profiled entries only` filter limits search, counts, and pagination to source records with a saved profile. Record details show raw source fields, associated root entry evidence, extraction output, profile concepts/tags/properties/claims, and embedding and pair-judgment status when available. The entry API also returns each extraction's identity status and reason; the Library continues to expose raw quarantined text for inspection, while MCP reads omit it. The library reads hosted PostgreSQL and does not download R2 snapshots or rerun inference. For a manual check, open the library, filter to profiled entries, search and advance a filtered page, inspect a profile, switch resources, then return to the map and open a connected node. The map is a pilot visualization, and group coordinates are recalculated within each displayed page rather than being stable global coordinates.
README.md:56:After extraction, `zsh run_parallel_profiles.sh` screens page identity, then runs four disjoint Kimi profile shards, waits for all to finish, and starts Cloudflare embeddings and the same 20,000-pair relation cap. Do not run it alongside `run_full.sh` or another profiling worker. It is safe to restart after interruption because screened pages and current profiles are skipped. If screening or any shard exits unsuccessfully, the script stops before embeddings and relations so the failed stage can be resumed deliberately.
README.md:64:`python3 -m mgraph evaluate` runs four fixed synthetic relation cases against GLM-5.3 and Kimi K3 and writes `evaluation.json`. This checks JSON validity, evidence IDs, proximity ordering, latency, and token cost. It is a smoke test, not a labeled corpus evaluation. A user-reviewed test set from actual Memotron entries is still needed before treating the scores as accuracy measurements.
